WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.815 --> 00:00:05.440
RUBEN VAN DER STOCKT:
My name is Ruben.

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:08.350
I work as a customer
engineer within Google Cloud,

00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:10.210
out of the Brussels office.

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:12.250
And the last three
and a half years, I

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:14.080
spent talking to a
lot of Enterprises

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:15.850
about Chrome and Android.

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:19.240
And in this session, I want
to talk to you about Chrome

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:21.010
and Android in the Enterprise.

00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:23.170
What we've achieved,
but also what's

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:28.150
next with our
mobility solutions.

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:31.450
We think of Android and
Chrome as the beginning point

00:00:31.450 --> 00:00:33.230
for a lot of our
Cloud solutions.

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:36.970
If you look at G Suite or any
application developed on Google

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:40.210
Cloud, they're best accessed
through Android and Chrome,

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:42.820
and we think that they
play well together.

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:45.640
Now, before we jump
into the presentation,

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:47.410
I just wanted to
give you the momentum

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:49.560
on both of these platforms.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:51.450
And it's quite
American to pull up

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:54.790
a slide with a lot of numbers,
but they're quite impressive.

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:58.960
If you look at Android, 1.4
billion 30-day active users

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:01.750
are using an Android
smartphone today.

00:01:01.750 --> 00:01:04.599
Consumers, Enterprise,
third world--

00:01:04.599 --> 00:01:07.760
a lot of people are
using Android today.

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:12.130
There's about 1.5 million
Android devices activated

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:15.010
daily, so the rate is still
going up on the Android

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:16.930
adoption in the world.

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:20.620
So about 82% of the
smartphones in the world

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:24.280
out there are
Android-based devices.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:28.390
If we look at the Enterprise
space, and the Android share

00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:32.320
is considerably lower
than the 82% figure.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:35.299
And we want to grow
this number, of course.

00:01:35.299 --> 00:01:37.090
We want to grow the
market share of Android

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:40.060
within the Enterprise
space with a larger number.

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:42.820
And we know we have a few
challenges to overcome that.

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:47.960
Security, patching issues, a
lot of diversity of devices.

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:50.130
If you a Samsung device
and the Sony device,

00:01:50.130 --> 00:01:51.782
they're completely different.

00:01:51.782 --> 00:01:53.740
So we have huge challenges
to overcome in order

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:56.230
to grow that market share.

00:01:56.230 --> 00:01:58.900
If we look at Chrome,
the first wave

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:01.810
of adoption of Chrome devices
was in the consumer space,

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:04.420
where even I gave
my mother a Chrome

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:07.810
device because every
time at Christmas,

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:11.420
she came to me to ask to
wipe her computer of all

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:14.210
of the viruses or a
software issue and so on.

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:18.700
I just gave her a Chrome device
with a Google mail solution

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:19.690
and it stopped.

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:23.170
Because basically all she
does is go into email,

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:26.590
do a few documents, calendar,
PC banking, and so on,

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:29.530
which is basically
all in the browser.

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:32.650
Second wave of adoption that we
saw is in the education space.

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:35.320
You might be aware of
the 52% market share

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:38.200
that we are getting in
the American public school

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:42.280
sector, where pupils are using
Chrome devices in order to get

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:43.990
teaching lessons and so on.

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:48.010
And what we've seen in the
last two to three years

00:02:48.010 --> 00:02:50.770
is an adoption of Chrome
devices in the Enterprise space,

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:53.500
as well, because of the
security and the simplicity

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:55.010
of the solution, as well.

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:56.950
And a lot of applications
today are already

00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:59.770
accessible through the
web browser, so why would

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:01.570
you still need a
[INAUDIBLE] client

00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:05.890
with all of the
management capabilities?

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:08.410
About 800,000
Chromebooks are sold

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:09.850
to do the business annually.

00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:14.710
And again, this is
definitely subject to growth.

00:03:14.710 --> 00:03:16.060
But the reality is much starker.

00:03:16.060 --> 00:03:19.200
If you look at the
total amount of workers,

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:22.330
3.25 billion workers
out there that

00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:25.600
are still not connected
to any sort of device

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:27.330
to sort of functionality.

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:29.440
There's 750 million
PCs in business

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:32.620
that need to be replaced and
about 300 million managed

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:34.070
smartphone devices.

00:03:34.070 --> 00:03:36.430
So there's definitely
an opportunity

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:39.700
for Chrome and Android
to grow into that space.

00:03:39.700 --> 00:03:41.270
Of course, we can't
do this alone.

00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:43.500
We do this with a
lot of our partners--

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:46.780
app developers,
OEMs, but also ISVs

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:49.210
that are writing,
specifically, applications

00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:53.440
for both of these platforms.

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:55.600
So what do I want to cover
in this presentation?

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:58.590
First of all, let's give you
an overview of the ecosystem.

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:01.460
What is the ecosystem that
both Android and Chrome

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:03.120
can benefit from?

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:06.150
Let's look at Android and Chrome
in the Enterprise specifically.

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:09.390
Device management and security
are notably the two strongest

00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:11.384
topics in this area.

00:04:11.384 --> 00:04:13.050
And last but not
least, let's finish off

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:15.600
with some best practices
and new functionality

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:19.014
coming up in the O version.

00:04:19.014 --> 00:04:20.220
The ecosystem.

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:22.800
I think the strongest
point on Android

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:25.560
is the application
developer community that

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:27.300
exists on Android.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:30.420
Android Studio is one of
the most used development

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:33.810
platforms for applications
for mobile applications.

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:36.840
If you look at Play,
65 billion applications

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:40.440
are installed as of today
through the Google Play

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:41.010
environment.

00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:45.120
And we basically offer a
good means for developers

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:46.860
to monetize on
their applications,

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:48.370
on their functionality.

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:51.630
But also for Enterprises to
develop specific applications

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:53.340
for their employees
and deliver them

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:55.070
through the Play ecosystem.

00:04:55.070 --> 00:04:58.770
1 million applications plus
applications are available.

00:04:58.770 --> 00:05:01.290
And of course,
these applications

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:04.480
are actively used today.

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:07.590
Today, this means that, through
the Google Play services,

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:10.210
you can deliver those
applications on smartphones,

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:14.490
tablets, and any other form
factor, like Google watches,

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:15.570
for example.

00:05:15.570 --> 00:05:18.810
But also, we've opened
up the Chrome ecosystem

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:23.320
to be able to run Play Services
on top of a Chrome device.

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:26.550
So this basically means
that a Chrome device

00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:28.740
capable of running Play
applications, Android

00:05:28.740 --> 00:05:31.050
applications, is
able to run these 1

00:05:31.050 --> 00:05:33.612
million available applications.

00:05:33.612 --> 00:05:35.070
And this is very
useful if you have

00:05:35.070 --> 00:05:38.430
offline capabilities-- if you
need offline capabilities.

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:41.330
For example, on my
work Chromebook,

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:43.080
I've enabled the Play store.

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:46.050
I downloaded the Netflix
Android applications

00:05:46.050 --> 00:05:49.680
and I'm able to watch a Netflix
series offline on my Chromebook

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:50.430
device.

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:54.600
But think of mail in a more
professional environment.

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:57.240
But this gives you a bit
of a better understanding

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:01.420
of the capabilities
of the Chrome devices.

00:06:01.420 --> 00:06:03.260
Applications are
very important to us,

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:07.290
and the huge success of Android
is also coming from the OEMs.

00:06:07.290 --> 00:06:09.150
We've taken a bit of
a different approach

00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:11.550
than our main
competitor in this area.

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:13.770
Where Android is
a very open system

00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:16.140
and can be adapted
by a lot of the OEMs

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:19.800
to provide a specific
experience on their phones.

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:23.640
Again, a Samsung device is quite
different in a user experience

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:25.830
than a Sony or Pixel device.

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:29.160
This openness really
drives the ecosystem,

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:31.380
but it gave us a lot
of challenges, as well.

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:34.230
Because, well, manageability
and also security

00:06:34.230 --> 00:06:38.040
becomes a very
difficult approach.

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:43.320
The most use case today is still
the smartphone and the tablet.

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:47.220
But if you have carriers
from DHL or PostNL,

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:50.250
they often use Android devices
to scan in your parcels,

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:51.030
as well.

00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:55.590
We see Android devices popping
up in hospitals, or just

00:06:55.590 --> 00:07:00.180
for nurses to be used for
their electronic patient files,

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:01.140
for example.

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:04.930
But we see the devices ecosystem
really growing, as well.

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:07.110
And we try to tackle
a lot of the issues

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:10.854
that we saw with the
diversity in this platform.

00:07:10.854 --> 00:07:11.770
We try to tackle them.

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:18.100
On the Chrome side, as well,
a lot of new form factors

00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:20.860
have been launched as of lately.

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:24.370
The majority of the use cases
that we saw for Chrome devices

00:07:24.370 --> 00:07:27.400
were in the connected
worker space.

00:07:27.400 --> 00:07:30.610
People using a Chrome device
to access functionality or use

00:07:30.610 --> 00:07:35.560
the browser in order to go
into their applications, what

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:37.180
we saw, as well,
is that Chrome is

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:39.970
an ideal platform for
single application purposes,

00:07:39.970 --> 00:07:42.460
like kiosks and so on.

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:44.800
The AOpen Chromebox
Mini, for example,

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:47.560
is a specifically
designed device

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:51.040
in order to run digital
signage or a single application

00:07:51.040 --> 00:07:52.200
for a very long time.

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:55.450
They're industry grade and
they're designed to run 24/7.

00:07:55.450 --> 00:07:59.315
And they're being used in
a lot of our Enterprises.

00:07:59.315 --> 00:08:03.340
The ASUS Chromebooks, for
example, is the PC form factor,

00:08:03.340 --> 00:08:06.700
and this is also being used
a lot in digital signage

00:08:06.700 --> 00:08:07.720
environments.

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:11.320
By the way, the largest
deployment of Chrome devices

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:14.650
is in Europe-- is
Toyota Motor Europe.

00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:16.760
So if you're entering
a showroom of Toyota

00:08:16.760 --> 00:08:19.390
and you see a large screen
with the latest cars,

00:08:19.390 --> 00:08:21.730
it's actually driven
by a Chrome device.

00:08:24.430 --> 00:08:28.310
Of course, there's a huge
ecosystem behind it, as well.

00:08:28.310 --> 00:08:30.580
And the one thing I
definitely want to mention

00:08:30.580 --> 00:08:31.780
is the management piece.

00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:34.690
We work very closely
together with the incumbents

00:08:34.690 --> 00:08:38.980
in the mobile device
management space like AirWatch,

00:08:38.980 --> 00:08:41.470
BlackBerry, MobileIron,
in order to make sure

00:08:41.470 --> 00:08:44.950
that all of these devices that
enter into the Enterprise space

00:08:44.950 --> 00:08:48.340
are managed in a proper way.

00:08:48.340 --> 00:08:50.590
The feedback that we've
gotten from these EMMs

00:08:50.590 --> 00:08:53.362
is that Android
is hard to manage.

00:08:53.362 --> 00:08:54.820
One of the hardest
things to manage

00:08:54.820 --> 00:08:57.850
is that there is no
consistent set of APIs

00:08:57.850 --> 00:08:59.730
available for Android devices.

00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:03.490
If you look at Lollipop, if you
look at KitKat, going further

00:09:03.490 --> 00:09:06.100
along the stream, there
was always a different way

00:09:06.100 --> 00:09:07.570
of managing these devices.

00:09:07.570 --> 00:09:11.350
And often, they need a
device owner rights in order

00:09:11.350 --> 00:09:12.910
to manage that device properly.

00:09:12.910 --> 00:09:15.040
Device owner rights
is a Windows term.

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:17.110
It's just administrator rights.

00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:20.560
You have access to
everything on that device.

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:22.280
We believe that
that's not secure.

00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:25.300
So in collaboration with
these EMM providers,

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:28.420
we've created a
specific set of APIs

00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:30.430
on which you can
manage the device

00:09:30.430 --> 00:09:34.810
in the various constellations.

00:09:34.810 --> 00:09:38.200
Whether that's a corporate-owned
single use case, device owner,

00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:41.630
or personal use.

00:09:41.630 --> 00:09:45.250
ISVs play a very
important role, as well.

00:09:45.250 --> 00:09:49.450
We see ISVs developing Android
Enterprise-specific features,

00:09:49.450 --> 00:09:50.230
as well.

00:09:50.230 --> 00:09:52.570
For example, sales force.

00:09:52.570 --> 00:09:55.150
If you deploy sales
force in a work profile,

00:09:55.150 --> 00:09:58.990
it can act differently than
a sales force application

00:09:58.990 --> 00:10:01.240
enrolled in a personal space.

00:10:01.240 --> 00:10:04.060
Very important aspect.

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:06.910
So I've been talking
about Android Enterprise.

00:10:06.910 --> 00:10:10.180
Let's look a bit at what
we're trying to achieve here.

00:10:10.180 --> 00:10:12.460
I think that the strongest
point about Android

00:10:12.460 --> 00:10:15.070
is that we're coming in
from a consumer angle.

00:10:15.070 --> 00:10:18.200
A lot of people are using
Android at home already

00:10:18.200 --> 00:10:21.400
and want to take that experience
into the Enterprise space.

00:10:21.400 --> 00:10:25.150
And this conflicts quite
a lot with the Enterprise

00:10:25.150 --> 00:10:27.160
organizations, or
organizations that

00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:31.120
want to standardize or
want a very secure solution

00:10:31.120 --> 00:10:32.320
in that place.

00:10:32.320 --> 00:10:35.260
If in a Bring Your
Own Device scenario,

00:10:35.260 --> 00:10:37.630
most of the organizations
demand to have

00:10:37.630 --> 00:10:40.120
full control over that device.

00:10:40.120 --> 00:10:44.680
Whether you want that or not,
it's quite often the case.

00:10:44.680 --> 00:10:47.500
What we are trying to do
with Android Enterprise

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:50.320
is basically build in
those capabilities in order

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:53.740
to allow organizations
to use Androids in a very

00:10:53.740 --> 00:10:56.810
secure and managed way.

00:10:56.810 --> 00:10:58.600
We started off with Lollipop.

00:10:58.600 --> 00:11:01.120
And for those who are
around for a longer time,

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:03.640
we called the name
Android for Work,

00:11:03.640 --> 00:11:05.670
basically bringing
Android for Work.

00:11:05.670 --> 00:11:10.000
But we decided to leave
the Android for Work part

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:11.080
and just keep it Android.

00:11:11.080 --> 00:11:13.600
Because it's basically
a standard set.

00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:16.390
If you buy a Pixel
phone today, Android

00:11:16.390 --> 00:11:18.850
for Work or the Android
Enterprise capabilities

00:11:18.850 --> 00:11:20.830
are built in.

00:11:20.830 --> 00:11:23.650
We started off with Lollipop.

00:11:23.650 --> 00:11:28.450
We gave the APIs, or
we described the APIs

00:11:28.450 --> 00:11:32.080
and recommended to be
used by the OEMs, which

00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:33.910
gave a bit of a
particular situation.

00:11:33.910 --> 00:11:36.310
Because some phones
support it and some phones

00:11:36.310 --> 00:11:39.889
didn't support it, so there
was a bit of ambiguity.

00:11:39.889 --> 00:11:40.930
So we didn't Marshmallow.

00:11:40.930 --> 00:11:45.220
We basically said, OK, if you
want to run Play Services,

00:11:45.220 --> 00:11:47.860
if you want to run Google
applications on top

00:11:47.860 --> 00:11:50.230
of your smartphone,
you need to fulfill

00:11:50.230 --> 00:11:52.120
all of the requirements
that are needed

00:11:52.120 --> 00:11:54.130
to run Android Enterprise.

00:11:54.130 --> 00:11:55.780
As a reminder,
Android Enterprise

00:11:55.780 --> 00:12:00.250
are a standard set of APIs
that can be used by an EMM

00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:04.960
to manage those
devices by a EMM.

00:12:04.960 --> 00:12:06.640
There were two use cases.

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:10.300
One was BYOD, so basically
bringing your own phone,

00:12:10.300 --> 00:12:13.240
adding a second profile, which
is managed by the organization.

00:12:13.240 --> 00:12:16.510
If you leave the organization,
you can wipe off that profile

00:12:16.510 --> 00:12:18.520
and still use the personal site.

00:12:18.520 --> 00:12:22.570
And we also offer the
corporate-owned version,

00:12:22.570 --> 00:12:23.360
as well.

00:12:23.360 --> 00:12:24.910
So basically, having
a phone that's

00:12:24.910 --> 00:12:27.550
been provided by the Enterprise
completely locked down

00:12:27.550 --> 00:12:29.170
for that aspect.

00:12:29.170 --> 00:12:33.160
In Nougat-- by the way,
we also added requirements

00:12:33.160 --> 00:12:35.280
for encryption
for these devices.

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:37.690
So any Marshmallow
device out there

00:12:37.690 --> 00:12:40.810
has a default encryption
built into those devices.

00:12:40.810 --> 00:12:44.240
Certainly from a security point
of view, that's very strong.

00:12:44.240 --> 00:12:47.110
Nougat, further usability
enhancement of management.

00:12:47.110 --> 00:12:50.590
There were a few hiccups with
regards to Identity Provision.

00:12:50.590 --> 00:12:54.340
That's all being solved and
it's very easy to be used.

00:12:54.340 --> 00:12:57.160
And of course, we are still
developing more and more

00:12:57.160 --> 00:13:02.200
into the O release coming up.

00:13:02.200 --> 00:13:04.810
What does it look like if you're
looking at a personal device

00:13:04.810 --> 00:13:06.870
from a work profile way?

00:13:06.870 --> 00:13:10.570
If you are using G Suite
today, this is already

00:13:10.570 --> 00:13:13.160
available for you to test out.

00:13:13.160 --> 00:13:15.640
So if you have a Marshmallow
and onwards Android

00:13:15.640 --> 00:13:20.010
device, what you are able to do
is activate the work profile.

00:13:20.010 --> 00:13:23.590
And what it basically does is
you have your personal phone,

00:13:23.590 --> 00:13:26.140
which is initiated with
your personal Gmail address,

00:13:26.140 --> 00:13:27.970
and you do whatever
you want on that phone

00:13:27.970 --> 00:13:29.900
because that's a personal space.

00:13:29.900 --> 00:13:32.770
But what you're adding
is a second profile

00:13:32.770 --> 00:13:35.140
on that device which
is completely managed

00:13:35.140 --> 00:13:36.700
by the organization.

00:13:36.700 --> 00:13:39.160
Organization can put
applications into that,

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:42.500
can also require, for example,
an additional pin code.

00:13:42.500 --> 00:13:45.580
So if you want to enter
this work profile,

00:13:45.580 --> 00:13:47.770
you need to enter
specific Pin code in order

00:13:47.770 --> 00:13:49.840
to access your
work applications.

00:13:49.840 --> 00:13:51.370
But what's really
neat about this

00:13:51.370 --> 00:13:56.050
is the complete separation
between personal and work data.

00:13:56.050 --> 00:13:58.300
From a user perspective,
my favorite feature

00:13:58.300 --> 00:14:01.510
of running this work profile is
that I can switch off the work

00:14:01.510 --> 00:14:03.100
profile when I'm at home.

00:14:03.100 --> 00:14:07.180
So I just drag down the action
list, tap on the work profile,

00:14:07.180 --> 00:14:10.000
and all of my work
applications are grayed out.

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:11.390
No notifications.

00:14:11.390 --> 00:14:14.290
And if I get an incoming call,
because my work profile is

00:14:14.290 --> 00:14:17.500
closed, it cannot
access my work contacts,

00:14:17.500 --> 00:14:21.190
so I know that that might be
somebody from work calling.

00:14:21.190 --> 00:14:26.140
I'm quite harsh on
my work-life balance.

00:14:26.140 --> 00:14:27.640
Also, it's
independently lockable.

00:14:27.640 --> 00:14:29.440
That's what I
explained, as well.

00:14:29.440 --> 00:14:33.820
And a company can
remotely wipe all

00:14:33.820 --> 00:14:36.670
of the contents of that
phone without touching

00:14:36.670 --> 00:14:40.520
the personal side
on that matter.

00:14:40.520 --> 00:14:43.280
If we look at
corporate-owned devices--

00:14:43.280 --> 00:14:45.890
these are devices
owned by corporate

00:14:45.890 --> 00:14:48.570
and then given to an end user--

00:14:48.570 --> 00:14:52.370
we do have some very good
management capabilities

00:14:52.370 --> 00:14:53.330
in there.

00:14:53.330 --> 00:14:55.370
Of course, the reset
wipe and managed

00:14:55.370 --> 00:14:57.470
reset protection is in there.

00:14:57.470 --> 00:15:02.270
If anybody has ever managed an
Android device and accidentally

00:15:02.270 --> 00:15:04.910
set up the factory
reset protection.

00:15:04.910 --> 00:15:10.970
So I have a customer who had 100
phones activated with one Gmail

00:15:10.970 --> 00:15:11.720
address.

00:15:11.720 --> 00:15:14.850
Some of them left and then
they wiped the whole device.

00:15:14.850 --> 00:15:16.700
If you want to
reuse that device,

00:15:16.700 --> 00:15:19.260
you need to have the password
of that personal account.

00:15:19.260 --> 00:15:22.100
So some of these
devices became lockable.

00:15:22.100 --> 00:15:26.090
With corporate-owned,
you absolutely lose that.

00:15:26.090 --> 00:15:29.660
You can also manage
the OTA update policy.

00:15:29.660 --> 00:15:31.740
So if you want to
have your devices

00:15:31.740 --> 00:15:35.310
receive the latest and
greatest on those devices--

00:15:35.310 --> 00:15:36.660
and we absolutely recommend it.

00:15:36.660 --> 00:15:38.870
So Google has done
a great effort

00:15:38.870 --> 00:15:41.510
to separate out the
functionality updates

00:15:41.510 --> 00:15:43.650
from the security updates.

00:15:43.650 --> 00:15:45.800
So functionality
updates need to go

00:15:45.800 --> 00:15:48.260
through a whole series
of testing by a carrier,

00:15:48.260 --> 00:15:52.160
by an OEM, before they can
be released to a phone.

00:15:52.160 --> 00:15:56.030
With security updates, we
have taken functionality

00:15:56.030 --> 00:15:58.970
away so that carriers
and OEMs can easily

00:15:58.970 --> 00:16:00.800
apply the security settings.

00:16:00.800 --> 00:16:03.140
And a lot of these
large corporations,

00:16:03.140 --> 00:16:06.830
like Samsung and BlackBerry, are
doing this for their flagship

00:16:06.830 --> 00:16:07.420
accounts.

00:16:07.420 --> 00:16:09.260
And we absolutely
recommend, if you're

00:16:09.260 --> 00:16:13.010
buying a large bulk of devices,
to have this as a requirement

00:16:13.010 --> 00:16:14.510
when you buy these devices.

00:16:18.370 --> 00:16:21.110
Another thing that
plays into mind

00:16:21.110 --> 00:16:23.430
when you're talking about
Enterprise capabilities--

00:16:23.430 --> 00:16:27.050
so you have the device
and default set of APIs.

00:16:27.050 --> 00:16:29.240
Next, what we're
offering in that space

00:16:29.240 --> 00:16:32.850
is the managed play environment.

00:16:32.850 --> 00:16:36.050
So as an organization,
you have a specific set

00:16:36.050 --> 00:16:39.230
of the Play Store available
in which you can whitelist

00:16:39.230 --> 00:16:43.580
the applications you want
to be using into that work

00:16:43.580 --> 00:16:44.870
environment.

00:16:44.870 --> 00:16:48.290
From a security point of
view, this is quite unique.

00:16:48.290 --> 00:16:51.360
Because you, as an
organization, decide

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:52.610
what applications are running.

00:16:52.610 --> 00:16:55.460
So you can make the assessment
whether an application

00:16:55.460 --> 00:16:58.620
is a potential harmful
application, yes or no.

00:16:58.620 --> 00:17:00.800
There's no other way
to install applications

00:17:00.800 --> 00:17:02.300
into a work profile.

00:17:02.300 --> 00:17:06.500
So that takes away
all of the risks

00:17:06.500 --> 00:17:09.170
of having a potential harmful
application into your work

00:17:09.170 --> 00:17:10.660
profile.

00:17:10.660 --> 00:17:13.099
The sources can be
public, so you don't have

00:17:13.099 --> 00:17:14.599
to do any application wrapping.

00:17:14.599 --> 00:17:16.579
You just select the
application that can

00:17:16.579 --> 00:17:20.030
run on an Enterprise profile.

00:17:20.030 --> 00:17:23.349
Or you can host your
APK at Google, or even

00:17:23.349 --> 00:17:24.829
at your own premises.

00:17:24.829 --> 00:17:28.940
But you use the Google Play
Store as the application

00:17:28.940 --> 00:17:32.570
installation, or application
delivery mechanism.

00:17:32.570 --> 00:17:35.810
Of course, there's a very
close connection with the EMMs

00:17:35.810 --> 00:17:38.390
so that you have a single
source where you all

00:17:38.390 --> 00:17:40.190
of this, of course.

00:17:40.190 --> 00:17:40.920
We deploy them.

00:17:40.920 --> 00:17:44.660
So the EMM, basically, you
whitelist the applications

00:17:44.660 --> 00:17:45.770
from an EMM point of view.

00:17:45.770 --> 00:17:47.570
You push the
applications, silently

00:17:47.570 --> 00:17:50.280
install these
applications onto a phone,

00:17:50.280 --> 00:17:53.480
or you can have the Play Store
as a self-service portal.

00:17:53.480 --> 00:17:57.770
So if end user wants to install
box or sales force or whatever,

00:17:57.770 --> 00:18:00.470
he's able to do that because
you, as an organization,

00:18:00.470 --> 00:18:01.790
are allowing this.

00:18:01.790 --> 00:18:03.440
Control the auto updates.

00:18:03.440 --> 00:18:06.860
And what's also very new
is applying application

00:18:06.860 --> 00:18:08.520
configurations to it, as well.

00:18:08.520 --> 00:18:10.940
So if you deploy a mail
client, you already

00:18:10.940 --> 00:18:13.650
give it a configuration
with that mail client

00:18:13.650 --> 00:18:15.530
so a user doesn't
have to fiddle around

00:18:15.530 --> 00:18:19.470
with any of the settings.

00:18:19.470 --> 00:18:21.960
What is less known
is that Google Play

00:18:21.960 --> 00:18:28.090
plays a very important aspect
in the security element.

00:18:28.090 --> 00:18:30.930
So if you're looking
at the Play Services,

00:18:30.930 --> 00:18:35.290
we basically do a lot on the
antivirus side of things.

00:18:35.290 --> 00:18:37.760
So if you look at the
Play applications host

00:18:37.760 --> 00:18:41.370
that within the Play store,
we do static controls.

00:18:41.370 --> 00:18:42.990
So we basically run--

00:18:42.990 --> 00:18:45.510
we check the code
base of an APK,

00:18:45.510 --> 00:18:49.480
but we also do dynamic testing
of all of these applications.

00:18:49.480 --> 00:18:51.600
So basically, running
these applications

00:18:51.600 --> 00:18:53.670
in a real-time
environment to check

00:18:53.670 --> 00:18:56.790
what an application is doing
and whether that application is

00:18:56.790 --> 00:18:59.160
exactly doing what
it says it does.

00:18:59.160 --> 00:19:02.190
In that case, we can
already take away

00:19:02.190 --> 00:19:04.440
a lot of these applications
that are basically

00:19:04.440 --> 00:19:09.070
camouflaging all of the
elements that they're doing.

00:19:09.070 --> 00:19:11.500
We also provide
device scans per day.

00:19:11.500 --> 00:19:13.410
So if you're an Android
user, we basically

00:19:13.410 --> 00:19:15.010
scan the device for you.

00:19:15.010 --> 00:19:18.750
There's a lot of anti-virus
applications on Android

00:19:18.750 --> 00:19:20.200
existing today.

00:19:20.200 --> 00:19:23.070
This is absolutely not necessary
because we take care of this,

00:19:23.070 --> 00:19:24.360
as well.

00:19:24.360 --> 00:19:27.300
What is less known
is that we also

00:19:27.300 --> 00:19:31.270
check the image of an
Android device, as well.

00:19:31.270 --> 00:19:33.970
So if you look at the
certification process,

00:19:33.970 --> 00:19:37.170
I said, well, if you want to
run the Play Services and Google

00:19:37.170 --> 00:19:39.330
applications on top
of an Android device,

00:19:39.330 --> 00:19:41.190
you need to fulfill
a certification

00:19:41.190 --> 00:19:43.290
or a set of requirements.

00:19:43.290 --> 00:19:45.060
Because we do that,
we scan the image,

00:19:45.060 --> 00:19:47.010
we know exactly
what the code base

00:19:47.010 --> 00:19:51.000
looks like of a
Samsung S6 out of 2014

00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:52.510
with carrier [INAUDIBLE].

00:19:52.510 --> 00:19:54.900
So we have a lot
of images available

00:19:54.900 --> 00:19:56.910
of specific configurations.

00:19:56.910 --> 00:20:00.060
If somebody tries to root
or has rooted his device,

00:20:00.060 --> 00:20:05.130
it has changed the code base
of the ROM of the system.

00:20:05.130 --> 00:20:07.650
So what we basically do
is we have an attestation

00:20:07.650 --> 00:20:11.970
API that can be called by, for
example, a banking application.

00:20:11.970 --> 00:20:14.430
So if the ING application
calls on this attestation

00:20:14.430 --> 00:20:16.980
application, it can
check whether the image

00:20:16.980 --> 00:20:20.580
that application is running on
is still to verify one or not.

00:20:20.580 --> 00:20:23.070
And based on that
information, you

00:20:23.070 --> 00:20:25.290
can take away the installation.

00:20:25.290 --> 00:20:30.470
So there's definitely a lot
happening on the background.

00:20:30.470 --> 00:20:32.160
Chrome OS.

00:20:32.160 --> 00:20:33.900
Again, this was always--

00:20:33.900 --> 00:20:36.630
so I focused on
the two platforms,

00:20:36.630 --> 00:20:38.810
so Chrome and Android
in the Enterprise space.

00:20:38.810 --> 00:20:41.850
There's two different
use cases for this.

00:20:41.850 --> 00:20:45.360
I had a lot of questions around
security on the Android side.

00:20:45.360 --> 00:20:49.710
With Chrome OS, I won the heart
of a lot of security officers,

00:20:49.710 --> 00:20:51.960
in such a sense that
some of the largest

00:20:51.960 --> 00:20:54.510
banks in the UK, the
security officers

00:20:54.510 --> 00:20:57.600
were running around with a
Chrome device accessing Citrix.

00:20:57.600 --> 00:21:00.220
Because on the end point,
there was absolutely no data.

00:21:00.220 --> 00:21:02.400
They just used the
Chrome device in order

00:21:02.400 --> 00:21:05.640
to access the information
on their own back end.

00:21:05.640 --> 00:21:08.550
And it was a very,
very strong point.

00:21:08.550 --> 00:21:12.050
We've always covered Chrome
was on all four topics.

00:21:12.050 --> 00:21:15.710
Speed, simplicity,
security, and shareability.

00:21:15.710 --> 00:21:17.430
Because of its very,
very warm here,

00:21:17.430 --> 00:21:19.670
I will not go into
a lot of details.

00:21:19.670 --> 00:21:22.230
We're all longing to
a good, cold drink.

00:21:22.230 --> 00:21:23.940
I am definitely one of them.

00:21:23.940 --> 00:21:26.990
But I just wanted to go
over them very quickly.

00:21:26.990 --> 00:21:28.350
Speed.

00:21:28.350 --> 00:21:29.940
If you've ever used
a Chrome device,

00:21:29.940 --> 00:21:33.829
it's just opening a lid, waiting
six seconds, start typing,

00:21:33.829 --> 00:21:35.370
and you have all of
the functionality

00:21:35.370 --> 00:21:36.930
that you ever need.

00:21:36.930 --> 00:21:39.570
I was a Mac user and before
that, I was a Windows user.

00:21:39.570 --> 00:21:41.820
There were times that
I opened up my laptop,

00:21:41.820 --> 00:21:43.920
pushed the power button,
went for a coffee

00:21:43.920 --> 00:21:49.170
and maybe I was in time
to type in my password.

00:21:49.170 --> 00:21:53.100
What we've done is we stripped
down a version of Linux

00:21:53.100 --> 00:21:57.010
and made it so that you can
only run a browser on top of it.

00:21:57.010 --> 00:21:59.490
And with every
release of Chrome OS,

00:21:59.490 --> 00:22:01.320
one thing that is
very important is

00:22:01.320 --> 00:22:05.280
that if we add
additional functionality,

00:22:05.280 --> 00:22:08.430
the Chrome OS needs to be
fostered in a previous version.

00:22:08.430 --> 00:22:11.140
If you take an old
Chromebook on version 24,

00:22:11.140 --> 00:22:14.400
update it to version
60 today, the device

00:22:14.400 --> 00:22:16.570
will guarantee it
will run faster,

00:22:16.570 --> 00:22:20.760
will run smoother, than before.

00:22:20.760 --> 00:22:21.420
Simple.

00:22:21.420 --> 00:22:23.920
I already gave the
example of my mother.

00:22:23.920 --> 00:22:25.620
The only thing she
needs to do is log in

00:22:25.620 --> 00:22:28.390
with her Gmail address
and she has access to it.

00:22:28.390 --> 00:22:31.620
And also, I've
been playing around

00:22:31.620 --> 00:22:33.480
with a lot of technology,
but the simplicity

00:22:33.480 --> 00:22:36.330
is still the one that
really grabs my mind.

00:22:36.330 --> 00:22:38.040
Secure, as well.

00:22:38.040 --> 00:22:39.630
Because it's a
browser, you can't

00:22:39.630 --> 00:22:43.800
install any software, any
DLLs, or any applications

00:22:43.800 --> 00:22:45.150
on top of it.

00:22:45.150 --> 00:22:46.710
Well, basically,
this takes away all

00:22:46.710 --> 00:22:49.320
of the potential
harmful applications.

00:22:49.320 --> 00:22:51.780
Basically, we are even
challenging the hacker

00:22:51.780 --> 00:22:56.600
community on Chrome OS
to hack into Chrome OS.

00:22:56.600 --> 00:22:59.490
If you find a vulnerability
and we verified it,

00:22:59.490 --> 00:23:04.600
we will pay you $100,000 in
order to prove this leak.

00:23:04.600 --> 00:23:09.040
And trust me, there is a lot
of people trying to do that.

00:23:09.040 --> 00:23:13.352
But today, the track
record is completely clean.

00:23:13.352 --> 00:23:15.300
Shareability.

00:23:15.300 --> 00:23:16.540
There is a lot of use cases.

00:23:16.540 --> 00:23:18.800
So if you look at the
information worker who

00:23:18.800 --> 00:23:21.310
has his own laptop or
his own Chromebook,

00:23:21.310 --> 00:23:24.110
shareability is not a big issue.

00:23:24.110 --> 00:23:26.390
But if, for example, my
Chromebook breaks down

00:23:26.390 --> 00:23:29.600
and I log in to my colleague's
one, that's perfectly fine.

00:23:29.600 --> 00:23:31.750
Because what you
do, every time you

00:23:31.750 --> 00:23:35.720
log into your Chrome device,
it creates a root directory,

00:23:35.720 --> 00:23:38.690
or it creates a directory for
the user, which is encrypted

00:23:38.690 --> 00:23:41.240
by that username and password.

00:23:41.240 --> 00:23:43.070
There's no notion of an admin.

00:23:43.070 --> 00:23:44.690
There's no notion
of somebody who

00:23:44.690 --> 00:23:47.930
can access that information
other than the user that

00:23:47.930 --> 00:23:50.360
has that username and password.

00:23:50.360 --> 00:23:53.330
What's very neat about
this shareability aspect is

00:23:53.330 --> 00:23:57.070
that, with a managed
environment-- so for example,

00:23:57.070 --> 00:23:59.570
we have a huge
project with a UK bank

00:23:59.570 --> 00:24:03.810
where employees who are logging
in with their bank account,

00:24:03.810 --> 00:24:05.630
it's a completely
lockdown environment.

00:24:05.630 --> 00:24:09.620
No video camera, no
microphones, no SD cards.

00:24:09.620 --> 00:24:11.787
Only Citrix can
run as a plug-in.

00:24:11.787 --> 00:24:13.120
That's the only thing it can do.

00:24:13.120 --> 00:24:14.280
It can access Citrix.

00:24:14.280 --> 00:24:14.990
That's it.

00:24:14.990 --> 00:24:17.810
But if he logs out, logs
in with his Gmail address,

00:24:17.810 --> 00:24:21.470
he has a full completely working
Chrome device in front of him.

00:24:21.470 --> 00:24:24.860
So highly regulated
industries, and also

00:24:24.860 --> 00:24:28.460
the freedom of using your
own username on that device.

00:24:28.460 --> 00:24:32.570
Of course, this is completely
manageable, as well.

00:24:32.570 --> 00:24:34.740
Let's talk about applications
on Chrome devices.

00:24:34.740 --> 00:24:38.710
And one of the strongest points,
or about 80% of a project

00:24:38.710 --> 00:24:40.300
I've been working
on, we're always

00:24:40.300 --> 00:24:43.140
related with
virtualization software.

00:24:43.140 --> 00:24:45.160
And we believe that
virtualization software

00:24:45.160 --> 00:24:49.510
is a stepping stone into a
fully Cloud-based workplace.

00:24:49.510 --> 00:24:52.120
And our end goal is, of course,
that all of the applications

00:24:52.120 --> 00:24:54.640
you access is through a browser.

00:24:54.640 --> 00:24:58.030
VMware in Citrix
and the other cloud,

00:24:58.030 --> 00:25:00.880
they have provided
some solutions in order

00:25:00.880 --> 00:25:04.000
to provide functionality
into a browser.

00:25:04.000 --> 00:25:06.329
Citrix and VMware are
the most advanced ones.

00:25:06.329 --> 00:25:07.870
And basically, what
you're able to do

00:25:07.870 --> 00:25:11.860
is swap out your thin clients
and use a Chrome device

00:25:11.860 --> 00:25:12.700
instead.

00:25:12.700 --> 00:25:14.530
And we freed up a
lot of employees

00:25:14.530 --> 00:25:18.220
from being tied behind a desk
with a thin client and one

00:25:18.220 --> 00:25:21.640
screen and a keyboard and
a mouse to a Chrome device

00:25:21.640 --> 00:25:26.110
that they can carry with
them in meeting rooms

00:25:26.110 --> 00:25:30.070
or can go into another office
with their own personal device.

00:25:30.070 --> 00:25:33.070
This works quite well.

00:25:33.070 --> 00:25:35.110
Of course, the one thing
that I didn't mention

00:25:35.110 --> 00:25:38.170
is the availability of
the play applications

00:25:38.170 --> 00:25:40.870
or the Android applications
on top of Chrome.

00:25:40.870 --> 00:25:45.370
What we've done here is a bit
of a specific implementation

00:25:45.370 --> 00:25:46.290
of containers.

00:25:46.290 --> 00:25:48.280
So there's basically
an Android device

00:25:48.280 --> 00:25:51.100
running on Chrome
bare metal, and we've

00:25:51.100 --> 00:25:55.450
intertwined the interface
so that it's really

00:25:55.450 --> 00:25:56.992
just one interface.

00:25:56.992 --> 00:25:58.450
So it doesn't matter
whether you're

00:25:58.450 --> 00:26:00.760
accessing a
Windows-based application

00:26:00.760 --> 00:26:04.330
through virtualization, a native
web application, or an Android

00:26:04.330 --> 00:26:04.870
application.

00:26:04.870 --> 00:26:08.497
It all looks the
same to the end user.

00:26:08.497 --> 00:26:10.330
What's particularly
interesting about Chrome

00:26:10.330 --> 00:26:13.300
is the connection with
the device management.

00:26:13.300 --> 00:26:15.100
And the device
management basically

00:26:15.100 --> 00:26:18.550
allows you to control any
aspect of a Chrome device.

00:26:18.550 --> 00:26:21.010
From the hardware sites,
so from devices side,

00:26:21.010 --> 00:26:24.310
from a remote point of view,
you can change the behavior

00:26:24.310 --> 00:26:26.960
of the device within seconds.

00:26:26.960 --> 00:26:30.250
So if you want to
say, OK, this person

00:26:30.250 --> 00:26:31.930
has moved into a
different role where

00:26:31.930 --> 00:26:35.230
he has less access
to applications,

00:26:35.230 --> 00:26:37.180
you can basically
limit that immediately

00:26:37.180 --> 00:26:40.300
by changing some
of these aspects.

00:26:40.300 --> 00:26:42.100
What's very nice,
as well, is that you

00:26:42.100 --> 00:26:46.080
can buy from any source, so any
commercially available Chrome

00:26:46.080 --> 00:26:47.440
device.

00:26:47.440 --> 00:26:50.680
If you buy them, they act
as just a consumer device.

00:26:50.680 --> 00:26:53.380
Once you've enrolled them
into the management console,

00:26:53.380 --> 00:26:55.730
you gain complete access to it.

00:26:55.730 --> 00:26:59.920
So configuring a device,
we've seen it in many cases,

00:26:59.920 --> 00:27:02.740
takes quite less time.

00:27:02.740 --> 00:27:07.000
Before that, they
took out the laptop,

00:27:07.000 --> 00:27:08.650
they installed a
specific image, they

00:27:08.650 --> 00:27:11.680
started to install software,
and then, after half a day,

00:27:11.680 --> 00:27:14.650
they can give the
laptop to an employee.

00:27:14.650 --> 00:27:17.120
Now in this case, they just
grab the Chrome device,

00:27:17.120 --> 00:27:21.430
tap Control-Alt-E, log in
with an admin or a user that

00:27:21.430 --> 00:27:24.620
is known within the device,
and you can ship that out

00:27:24.620 --> 00:27:26.680
to an end user.

00:27:26.680 --> 00:27:29.740
Because in that case, this
is completely managed.

00:27:29.740 --> 00:27:31.690
Remote lock and ultimate
security, of course.

00:27:31.690 --> 00:27:34.690
So if you leave a Chrome
device on the train,

00:27:34.690 --> 00:27:37.450
well, the data on that
device is already minimal

00:27:37.450 --> 00:27:41.200
because you use the browser
and downloading software,

00:27:41.200 --> 00:27:43.900
downloading data is
not really happening.

00:27:43.900 --> 00:27:47.740
But using the encryption
on the user level,

00:27:47.740 --> 00:27:51.940
a data thief cannot get access
to that from an encryption

00:27:51.940 --> 00:27:52.870
point of view.

00:27:52.870 --> 00:27:55.840
And even if he tries to
take out the hard disk

00:27:55.840 --> 00:27:59.200
and try to put in another hard
disk into that Chrome device,

00:27:59.200 --> 00:28:01.480
the device won't boot
because the hard disk

00:28:01.480 --> 00:28:05.230
is known as a known
configuration within Chrome OS.

00:28:05.230 --> 00:28:09.130
It's very complex, in that case.

00:28:11.650 --> 00:28:13.020
Native security hardware.

00:28:13.020 --> 00:28:15.160
In every Chrome
device, there's a TPM--

00:28:15.160 --> 00:28:17.260
trusted platform model chip.

00:28:17.260 --> 00:28:19.799
We provide the operating
system and updates.

00:28:19.799 --> 00:28:22.090
Again, a bit different than
the Android site, and we're

00:28:22.090 --> 00:28:24.850
trying to copy a lot
of the Chrome site

00:28:24.850 --> 00:28:26.260
into the Android site.

00:28:26.260 --> 00:28:28.354
So we, as Google,
every six weeks,

00:28:28.354 --> 00:28:29.770
there's a new
version of Chrome OS

00:28:29.770 --> 00:28:32.180
no matter what kind of
device you're using,

00:28:32.180 --> 00:28:35.800
whether that's a Chromebook
Flip, a Chromebit,

00:28:35.800 --> 00:28:38.530
or a Chromebase,
it's one codebase.

00:28:38.530 --> 00:28:40.960
It's just one operating system.

00:28:40.960 --> 00:28:44.770
We have a profound use
of sandboxing, as well.

00:28:44.770 --> 00:28:47.680
Basically meaning that every
service runs in its own sandbox

00:28:47.680 --> 00:28:49.960
and can only give
access or has access

00:28:49.960 --> 00:28:53.080
to certain data
particles or programs

00:28:53.080 --> 00:28:59.670
on the device by policy.

00:28:59.670 --> 00:29:02.670
So let's look at a few
of the best practices.

00:29:02.670 --> 00:29:05.735
If we're looking at
mobile device management--

00:29:05.735 --> 00:29:08.860
or mobile devices--
we see for access.

00:29:08.860 --> 00:29:11.140
And let's start in
the left lower corner

00:29:11.140 --> 00:29:13.220
with an unmanaged device.

00:29:13.220 --> 00:29:15.280
An unmanaged device is
just a feature phone

00:29:15.280 --> 00:29:17.830
or a phone that's being
used and is not connected

00:29:17.830 --> 00:29:19.780
to the Enterprise in any way.

00:29:19.780 --> 00:29:21.970
One abstract layer
on top of that

00:29:21.970 --> 00:29:27.190
is basically the personal
usage of a device,

00:29:27.190 --> 00:29:29.330
adding a work
profile on top of it.

00:29:29.330 --> 00:29:31.330
Now, the best
practice in this is

00:29:31.330 --> 00:29:34.120
that the minimal requirement
that we recommend

00:29:34.120 --> 00:29:35.500
is Marshmallow.

00:29:35.500 --> 00:29:39.670
Marshmallow devices have
Android Enterprise capabilities

00:29:39.670 --> 00:29:45.040
built in, have that Android
work profile, by default,

00:29:45.040 --> 00:29:46.540
in their APIs.

00:29:46.540 --> 00:29:48.940
So this basically
allows organizations

00:29:48.940 --> 00:29:53.650
to have the EMM manage that
work profile on top of it.

00:29:53.650 --> 00:29:56.455
The second layer on top of
that is a managed device.

00:29:56.455 --> 00:30:00.280
This is a corporate-owned,
personal enabled device.

00:30:00.280 --> 00:30:02.980
Basically, the organization
or the employer

00:30:02.980 --> 00:30:05.770
is in charge of this device.

00:30:05.770 --> 00:30:10.360
Again, Marshmallow is the
least operating system

00:30:10.360 --> 00:30:12.430
that we recommend
in that case because

00:30:12.430 --> 00:30:14.650
of the standard
encryption, and also

00:30:14.650 --> 00:30:18.820
the addition of the
default APIs in that case.

00:30:18.820 --> 00:30:21.070
Last but not least,
the dedicated devices

00:30:21.070 --> 00:30:23.920
within the Android
Enterprise capabilities,

00:30:23.920 --> 00:30:27.490
we've also added kiosk
usage into the Android

00:30:27.490 --> 00:30:28.930
images and the APIs.

00:30:28.930 --> 00:30:31.810
Basically, giving a
consistent management

00:30:31.810 --> 00:30:34.050
of single applications
on those devices.

00:30:34.050 --> 00:30:38.310
So think of a tablet running
just one single application

00:30:38.310 --> 00:30:41.650
at kiosks in shops, for
example, or for digital signage.

00:30:41.650 --> 00:30:42.940
This is added, as well.

00:30:46.290 --> 00:30:49.820
Do not use unknown sources
and force verify apps.

00:30:49.820 --> 00:30:55.080
There's still a major EMM
that requires the user

00:30:55.080 --> 00:30:59.490
to turn off this feature.

00:30:59.490 --> 00:31:02.870
If you ever download it from
Chrome browser or any browser

00:31:02.870 --> 00:31:06.090
APK, if you try to
install that on Android,

00:31:06.090 --> 00:31:08.050
it will give you a
Warning saying, hey,

00:31:08.050 --> 00:31:11.340
you cannot install this
because this is an unverified

00:31:11.340 --> 00:31:12.780
application.

00:31:12.780 --> 00:31:15.510
We always recommend
installing applications

00:31:15.510 --> 00:31:19.140
from the Play Store because of
the reasons I just explained.

00:31:19.140 --> 00:31:21.570
We do a lot of
research, we do a lot

00:31:21.570 --> 00:31:24.370
of testing on the applications.

00:31:24.370 --> 00:31:26.580
So we think the
Play and, certainly,

00:31:26.580 --> 00:31:30.270
the whitelisted applications in
Play, are the most secure way

00:31:30.270 --> 00:31:32.910
to use applications.

00:31:32.910 --> 00:31:36.720
So please do not set the policy
that unknown sources cannot be

00:31:36.720 --> 00:31:37.350
switched off.

00:31:37.350 --> 00:31:39.210
It's a default policy.

00:31:39.210 --> 00:31:40.500
Required device encryption.

00:31:40.500 --> 00:31:42.240
Of course, I cannot
stress on this.

00:31:42.240 --> 00:31:44.400
If you leave your
phone on the train,

00:31:44.400 --> 00:31:47.580
you want to make sure that the
device is encrypted by default.

00:31:47.580 --> 00:31:49.580
And we've done a
lot of work in order

00:31:49.580 --> 00:31:54.890
to make sure that personal and
business data is separately

00:31:54.890 --> 00:31:57.640
encrypted, as well.

00:31:57.640 --> 00:31:59.820
Enforced minimal
security update policies.

00:31:59.820 --> 00:32:03.070
Again, if you're buying
a lot of devices,

00:32:03.070 --> 00:32:05.370
please make sure that the
OEM you are buying from

00:32:05.370 --> 00:32:09.540
is guaranteeing that you get
monthly security updates.

00:32:09.540 --> 00:32:12.670
We try to be the best
pupil in the class,

00:32:12.670 --> 00:32:14.885
so if you buy a Pixel
phone, we guarantee

00:32:14.885 --> 00:32:18.090
that, at least two years, you
will receive monthly security

00:32:18.090 --> 00:32:20.670
updates for these
devices, as well.

00:32:20.670 --> 00:32:22.860
And a lot of the OEMs are
following this, as well,

00:32:22.860 --> 00:32:25.140
because they see
that security is very

00:32:25.140 --> 00:32:27.930
important in a lot of cases.

00:32:27.930 --> 00:32:31.410
Separate management of
work and personal data.

00:32:31.410 --> 00:32:34.860
Again, from a user perspective,
personally, I quite

00:32:34.860 --> 00:32:37.230
like the separation of
work and personal data.

00:32:37.230 --> 00:32:40.290
Because I know that what
I do in my personal set

00:32:40.290 --> 00:32:45.360
is completely separated from
my data from my work profile.

00:32:45.360 --> 00:32:48.150
Again, if you're working with
a lot of photos, some photos

00:32:48.150 --> 00:32:51.750
you don't want to end up in
the work profile, of course.

00:32:51.750 --> 00:32:55.690
I use the latest
available Management APIs.

00:32:55.690 --> 00:32:58.035
So what's next?

00:32:58.035 --> 00:32:58.660
So what's next?

00:32:58.660 --> 00:33:01.550
I've talked about
a few use cases.

00:33:01.550 --> 00:33:03.880
So Bring Your Own Device.

00:33:03.880 --> 00:33:06.280
You have your personal
profile with a work profile

00:33:06.280 --> 00:33:08.830
on top of it, which is
managed by the organization.

00:33:08.830 --> 00:33:12.040
Well, some of our
customers said, we

00:33:12.040 --> 00:33:13.550
want it the other way around.

00:33:13.550 --> 00:33:16.750
We have a corporate-owned
device and we

00:33:16.750 --> 00:33:19.900
want to enable a
personal profile to it so

00:33:19.900 --> 00:33:22.960
that the user can still
do it, but we remain 100%

00:33:22.960 --> 00:33:26.330
in control over
the complete phone.

00:33:26.330 --> 00:33:28.960
So that's something that
we are launching in O.

00:33:28.960 --> 00:33:32.140
So this will
basically allow users

00:33:32.140 --> 00:33:34.700
to add their own
personal profile for it

00:33:34.700 --> 00:33:36.550
in a corporate-owned
device, still

00:33:36.550 --> 00:33:40.420
maintaining the whole data
separation on top of it.

00:33:40.420 --> 00:33:43.660
We are probably not using
the work profile icon.

00:33:43.660 --> 00:33:45.695
It will be something
different because then it's

00:33:45.695 --> 00:33:46.570
the other way around.

00:33:46.570 --> 00:33:48.610
It might be
confusing if you want

00:33:48.610 --> 00:33:52.000
to access personal information
through an application that

00:33:52.000 --> 00:33:53.020
has a work batch on it.

00:33:53.020 --> 00:33:58.090
So that will probably go away.

00:33:58.090 --> 00:34:00.880
What we're doing in O,
as well, is separately

00:34:00.880 --> 00:34:06.160
encrypt the data from the work
profile and the personal space.

00:34:06.160 --> 00:34:09.429
Again, in case you want to
wipe a device, in that case,

00:34:09.429 --> 00:34:13.600
you just have to throw away the
encryption key of the work site

00:34:13.600 --> 00:34:17.469
so that any application or
any data cannot be accessed

00:34:17.469 --> 00:34:19.830
afterwards.

00:34:19.830 --> 00:34:23.540
We are adding new privacy
notices on corporate devices.

00:34:23.540 --> 00:34:28.080
A huge feedback because if
you're working on a work

00:34:28.080 --> 00:34:29.986
managed or a work
profile device,

00:34:29.986 --> 00:34:31.860
some of the capabilities
on the personal side

00:34:31.860 --> 00:34:33.510
can be switched off, as well.

00:34:33.510 --> 00:34:38.469
We've added the capabilities to
add some help notes, as well.

00:34:38.469 --> 00:34:40.380
So basically saying,
OK, your corporation

00:34:40.380 --> 00:34:43.050
has switched of this
setting because of this.

00:34:43.050 --> 00:34:48.270
Or you can contact
your organization.

00:34:48.270 --> 00:34:50.409
This is quite handy, as well.

00:34:50.409 --> 00:34:53.400
So basically, if you lose a
phone, in Android, by default,

00:34:53.400 --> 00:34:56.280
you're able to say, OK, this
phone is for me with a phone

00:34:56.280 --> 00:34:58.890
number so that people can
return the phone if they

00:34:58.890 --> 00:35:00.210
haven't stolen it.

00:35:00.210 --> 00:35:02.800
This can be set by
corporate policy, as well.

00:35:02.800 --> 00:35:05.490
So basically saying, OK,
this is the service desk.

00:35:05.490 --> 00:35:10.800
If you find this phone, please
return it to this organization.

00:35:10.800 --> 00:35:12.570
Device provisioning.

00:35:12.570 --> 00:35:13.450
The second topic.

00:35:13.450 --> 00:35:14.655
So security in Android.

00:35:14.655 --> 00:35:17.520
The second topic was
device provisioning.

00:35:17.520 --> 00:35:20.540
The other phone
manufacturer out there

00:35:20.540 --> 00:35:24.420
that's competing with
Android has a very, very neat

00:35:24.420 --> 00:35:25.110
mechanism.

00:35:25.110 --> 00:35:28.470
You just provide the serial
number and once a user

00:35:28.470 --> 00:35:31.860
opens up or boots that phone,
it's automatically enrolled

00:35:31.860 --> 00:35:35.246
within the EMM, for example.

00:35:35.246 --> 00:35:37.160
With Android, that's
quite different

00:35:37.160 --> 00:35:39.736
because we don't own the
hardware of that device.

00:35:39.736 --> 00:35:41.610
We don't know what the
device is and we don't

00:35:41.610 --> 00:35:44.520
know who it's attached to.

00:35:44.520 --> 00:35:46.860
We are trying to work
on a way in order

00:35:46.860 --> 00:35:47.860
to mimic that situation.

00:35:47.860 --> 00:35:50.130
And you will see that
with Pixel phones already.

00:35:50.130 --> 00:35:53.040
If you buy a Pixel
phone in bulk,

00:35:53.040 --> 00:35:56.250
you get a set of
IMEI numbers, which

00:35:56.250 --> 00:35:57.810
you can upload into a portal.

00:35:57.810 --> 00:36:00.240
And once that device
boots up, the device

00:36:00.240 --> 00:36:04.650
will be automatically enrolled
within the mobile device

00:36:04.650 --> 00:36:06.030
management console.

00:36:06.030 --> 00:36:08.050
We're working on
QR codes, as well.

00:36:08.050 --> 00:36:10.620
So if you can scan a code, then
it will automatically enroll.

00:36:10.620 --> 00:36:12.690
NFC tokens, as well,
is something that's

00:36:12.690 --> 00:36:14.760
available in the near future.

00:36:17.930 --> 00:36:20.180
Work profile improvements.

00:36:20.180 --> 00:36:22.510
Verify Apps strict mode.

00:36:22.510 --> 00:36:24.500
Stricter enforcement
of verify applications,

00:36:24.500 --> 00:36:27.370
and verify applications
is our antivirus solution.

00:36:27.370 --> 00:36:30.230
So we're extending
that functionality

00:36:30.230 --> 00:36:31.730
in a lot more phones.

00:36:31.730 --> 00:36:33.500
So basically, able
to scan those phones

00:36:33.500 --> 00:36:35.160
at any time at any place.

00:36:35.160 --> 00:36:37.310
And new APIs will be
available, as well.

00:36:41.504 --> 00:36:44.370
I basically mentioned
this, as well.

00:36:44.370 --> 00:36:46.590
On the Chrome site,
this is a device

00:36:46.590 --> 00:36:50.110
that will come out in
Europe quickly, as well.

00:36:50.110 --> 00:36:52.104
This is the new
flagship Chrome device.

00:36:52.104 --> 00:36:53.520
This is the Samsung
Chrome device.

00:36:53.520 --> 00:36:55.740
They're re-entering
the Chrome market.

00:36:55.740 --> 00:36:59.500
And this is a premium,
two-in-one Chromebook offering.

00:36:59.500 --> 00:37:01.620
We often get the question,
like, you have Android

00:37:01.620 --> 00:37:03.390
and you have Chrome
as a platform.

00:37:03.390 --> 00:37:05.260
Why maintain both?

00:37:05.260 --> 00:37:08.760
Well, we think that Android
is great for mobility

00:37:08.760 --> 00:37:11.880
for internet of things
for smaller devices

00:37:11.880 --> 00:37:13.650
to consume information.

00:37:13.650 --> 00:37:16.314
We think Chrome is more of
a productivity platform.

00:37:16.314 --> 00:37:17.730
And what you will
be seeing, there

00:37:17.730 --> 00:37:21.160
was a huge strict separation
between tablets and Chrome

00:37:21.160 --> 00:37:21.660
devices.

00:37:21.660 --> 00:37:23.460
There was never a Chrome tablet.

00:37:23.460 --> 00:37:27.460
We are now investigating, or
re-investigating, that aspect.

00:37:27.460 --> 00:37:31.060
So in the first phase, you will
see this sort of convertible.

00:37:31.060 --> 00:37:33.630
So you can use this as a tablet,
and we've done a lot of work

00:37:33.630 --> 00:37:38.280
to have the stylus working
very well on a Chrome device.

00:37:38.280 --> 00:37:40.050
I see, in the near
future, that you

00:37:40.050 --> 00:37:45.030
could work on some detachables,
or even on a Chrome device.

00:37:45.030 --> 00:37:47.130
And this is very
useful in kiosk usage

00:37:47.130 --> 00:37:49.320
because you have a very
strong, secure device.

00:37:49.320 --> 00:37:50.730
It's a very shareable device.

00:37:50.730 --> 00:37:54.795
But still have that mobility
grasp, as well, with Chrome OS.

00:37:54.795 --> 00:37:57.420
It's quite a nice device.

00:37:57.420 --> 00:38:00.120
A big announcement, as
well, is with VMware.

00:38:00.120 --> 00:38:02.815
So VMware offers
virtualization software,

00:38:02.815 --> 00:38:05.190
so you have virtual applications
that can run on a Chrome

00:38:05.190 --> 00:38:07.020
device, as well.

00:38:07.020 --> 00:38:08.970
The Chrome device
management piece

00:38:08.970 --> 00:38:11.380
was something you could
only buy from Google.

00:38:11.380 --> 00:38:14.370
So you buy a license and you
can manage your Chrome device.

00:38:14.370 --> 00:38:17.790
What we've done is we've opened
up that device management

00:38:17.790 --> 00:38:20.580
capabilities by
opening up the APIs.

00:38:20.580 --> 00:38:24.780
And what VMware has done is they
have incorporated these APIs

00:38:24.780 --> 00:38:27.670
into their device
management capabilities.

00:38:27.670 --> 00:38:33.600
So if you already have a VMware
workspace, one environment,

00:38:33.600 --> 00:38:36.600
you now can manage these
devices through that workspace,

00:38:36.600 --> 00:38:37.380
as well.

00:38:37.380 --> 00:38:39.480
And we see a lot of
new things popping up,

00:38:39.480 --> 00:38:42.870
as well, new ways of
managing devices, as well.

00:38:42.870 --> 00:38:45.710
Active Directory, for
example, is a lot used

00:38:45.710 --> 00:38:47.460
within the Enterprise space.

00:38:47.460 --> 00:38:51.030
A lot of people are not yet
in the Google ecosystem,

00:38:51.030 --> 00:38:54.360
so we're looking
for simpler ways

00:38:54.360 --> 00:38:56.910
of managing Chrome devices
out of the Active Directory,

00:38:56.910 --> 00:39:04.430
for example, opening up
a lot of new use cases.

00:39:04.430 --> 00:39:07.730
So to conclude the
session, I hope

00:39:07.730 --> 00:39:09.870
it was useful, first of all.

00:39:09.870 --> 00:39:15.590
Thanks for staying in the room,
even when it's now 36 degrees.

00:39:15.590 --> 00:39:18.440
If you want to test drive the
Android management experience,

00:39:18.440 --> 00:39:21.620
or if you want to
verify what your EMM is

00:39:21.620 --> 00:39:25.190
telling is the truth, there's
the Android management

00:39:25.190 --> 00:39:25.860
experience.

00:39:25.860 --> 00:39:27.650
So if you look at
this link, and I

00:39:27.650 --> 00:39:30.270
think these slides
will be shared,

00:39:30.270 --> 00:39:34.460
it basically allows you to have
the managed Android experience

00:39:34.460 --> 00:39:38.280
without setting up all of
the management capabilities.

00:39:38.280 --> 00:39:44.260
So it's a very useful way to
test out all of these APIs.

00:39:44.260 --> 00:39:47.415
And if you're really interested
in the connected workspaces,

00:39:47.415 --> 00:39:50.750
in where the cloud
meets the devices,

00:39:50.750 --> 00:39:55.130
I invite you to look at this
connected workspaces page,

00:39:55.130 --> 00:39:57.230
as well, which
shows, a bit more,

00:39:57.230 --> 00:39:58.970
the capabilities
of what's possible,

00:39:58.970 --> 00:40:02.620
what's next with these devices.

00:40:02.620 --> 00:40:06.710
So thank you very much
and hope you enjoyed it.

00:40:06.710 --> 00:40:08.540
And let's go for a drink.

00:40:08.540 --> 00:40:11.890
[APPLAUSE]

